BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20260627T190000Z
DTEND:20260627T200000Z
DTSTAMP:20260627T000000Z
SUMMARY:Justice in the Weeds: Why People Reclaim Neglected Cemeteries
DESCRIPTION:Across the United States\, people are coming together to care 
 for overgrown cemeteries—the burial grounds of marginalized people\, 
 including African Americans\, psychiatric hospital patients\, and the poor. 
 These volunteer groups pull up weeds\, clean headstones\, and uncover lost 
 stories. But they are doing more than yard work. They are raising big 
 questions: Why were these cemeteries neglected in the first place? What 
 does it say about how some lives—and deaths—are treated as less 
 valuable than others? And what can we do\, after people’s deaths\, to 
 show them respect and care?\n\nIn this talk\, Professor Adam Rosenblatt 
 shares stories from his book Cemetery Citizens\, which explores cemetery 
 reclamation efforts in Durham\, Richmond\, Philadelphia\, and other places. 
 You’ll hear about the challenges volunteers face\, the powerful emotions 
 that come with uncovering forgotten histories\, and how caring for the dead 
 can help build new kinds of community. The talk also invites discussion: 
 what does justice for the dead look like—and what can it mean for the 
 living?\n\n\nAbout the Presenter:\n\nAdam Rosenblatt is the Faculty 
 Director of the Duke Human Rights Center and Professor of International 
 Comparative Studies and Cultural Anthropology at Duke. He is the author of 
 Digging for the Disappeared: Forensic Science after Atrocity and Cemetery 
 Citizens: Reclaiming the Past and Working for Justice in American Burial 
 Grounds. He is also a cartoonist and zine-maker.
LOCATION:East Meeting Room\, East Regional Library (211 Lick Creek Lane)
ORGANIZER;CN="Mara Plantholt":MAILTO:mplantholt@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:Classes & Lectures, Humanities, Summer Reading
UID:LibCal-14288-16603251
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/16603251
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20260627T190000Z
DTEND:20260627T203000Z
DTSTAMP:20260627T000000Z
SUMMARY:Another Family Against the Amendment - The Story of the People's Alliance Campaign against Amendment One in Durham
DESCRIPTION:In 2012 the NC State Legislature proposed Amendment One. This 
 was a change to the NC Constitution and prohibited the state from 
 recognizing or performing same-sex marriages\, civil unions or civil union 
 equivalents by defining male–female marriage as "the only domestic legal 
 union" considered valid or recognized in the state. \n\nIn recognition of 
 the 50th anniversary of the People's Alliance\, join a panel of organizers 
 and elected officials as they tell the history of the local reaction to 
 Amendment One. \n\nPanelists:\n\nKate Fellman: Executive Director of You 
 Can Vote\,  PA Staff member during the Amendment One campaign\n\nWendy 
 Jacobs: Durham County Commissioner\, served on the Durham BOCC and first 
 ran for election during the Amendment One campaign \n\nSailor Jones: State 
 Director\, Common Cause\, statewide lead of Amendment One campaign\n\nSara 
 Terry: Long time Durham resident and advocate\, PA Board lead during the 
 campaign\n\nJake Terry-Edmunds: Moderator\, Clinical Research Coordinator 
 at UNC School of Medicine
LOCATION:Main Auditorium\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="Lauren Panny":MAILTO:lpanny@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:North Carolina Collection
UID:LibCal-14288-16958624
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/16958624
END:VEVENT
